CSO Housing App — Meeting Notes
1. CSO Housing App
Objective
Create a housing app that allows graduate students to submit 11-54 requests directly to the CSO Graduate Housing Office instead of calling the CSO phone line while CSOs record information by hand.
Current Process
- CSOs sit in the office and record discrepancy information by hand.
- There is no digital way to track information or generate statistics from 11-54 requests.
- Communication occurs only by phone.
- Students do not receive live updates about their request or place in line.
Proposed Process
- Students and their families, roommates, authorized individuals, or sublessees can access the appropriate app portal and submit 11-54 requests.
- CSOs use the Mercury platform to verify residency using information such as the user’s full name and student ID.
- CSOs call a separate phone number to verify the credentials of users who cannot be verified through Mercury, such as family members or sublessees.
Access and Authentication
- Authentication is required for users.
- The system should support different authorization levels:
  - Users: Create, view, edit, and delete their own requests or reservations.
  - CSO administrators: Full create, read, update, and delete access to all requests and records.
- The system should support verification for students as well as family members, authorized individuals, roommates, and sublessees.
Queue and Status Features
- Display a live waiting queue.
- Show how many callouts are ahead of each request.
- Provide real-time updates on a user’s position in line.
- Provide an estimated wait time.
- Notify CSOs when new people enter the queue.
- Allow users to view the current status of their request.
2. Schedule and Sign-In Form Community Monitor
- Research an inexpensive computer or touchscreen wall display for CSOs to use to:
  - View the weekly WhenToWork schedule.
  - Sign in to the CSO Sign In App.
- Target budget: $500 or less.
- Restrict the computer to these features to prevent unauthorized use.
- Investigate whether the device can be wall-mounted.
3. Other Notes
- New emails will be sent to schedule access on WhenToWork.
- Hours must be manually logged on the schedule under the label “PD: IT.”
- Review the Mercury demonstration video.
4. Metrics and Data Tracking
Use a Google Sheet or another suitable database to track 11-54 request information, statuses, and performance metrics.
Metrics to Track
- Number of completed requests.
- Response time.
- Number of incoming calls.
- Number of requests that were not completed.
The system should preserve the exact information needed for CSO operations, reporting, and potential billing or service records.
5. Implementation Questions
- What verification method should be used for students, family members, authorized individuals, roommates, and sublessees?
- Is single sign-on (SSO) available or required for users?
- How can the system verify that a user has graduate student status?
- What functionality is needed to add a family member or authorized individual to a request?
- What exact information must users submit for CSOs to verify residency?
- If a mobile app is released, who will pay for the Apple Developer Program fee of $99 per year and the Android developer registration fee of $25 one time?
- Is funding available for mobile app development and ongoing platform costs?
6. Platform Requirements
- Android app.
- Apple/iOS app.
- Web version.
- User-facing version with the ability to:
  - Submit and view requests.
  - Edit or delete the user’s own reservation or request.
  - View request status.
  - View queue position and estimated wait time.
- CSO-facing version with expanded permissions to:
  - View all requests.
  - Create, edit, and delete records.
  - Manage the live queue.
  - Update request statuses.
  - View operational metrics.
  - Receive notifications when users enter the queue.
